<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">
  <PropertyGroup>
    <OutputType>Exe</OutputType>
    <RootNamespace>Fuzzy.Text.RegularExpressions.Tests</RootNamespace>
    <IsPackable>false</IsPackable>
    <IsTestProject>true</IsTestProject>

    <!--
      S53. Microsoft.Testing.Platform ships localized satellite assemblies (cs, de, es, fr, ...).
      Native AOT compiles them into the binary, where they show up in AppDomain.GetAssemblies()
      carrying a CultureName - and AwesomeAssertions walks that list to find the test framework's
      exception type, which means constructing CultureInfo("cs"). Under the repo-wide
      InvariantGlobalization=true that throws, so EVERY assertion failure in the published binary
      was reported as "CultureNotFoundException: cs is an invalid culture identifier" instead of as
      the failure - measured 2026-09-16, and it would have made the AOT gate useless as a
      diagnostic. Shipping only English resources is the fix that keeps InvariantGlobalization
      honest; the alternative was turning globalization on for the test app alone, which changes
      the environment the library is tested in.
    -->
    <SatelliteResourceLanguages>en</SatelliteResourceLanguages>

    <!--
      S53. This project is published with PublishAot=true and the produced executable is run as the
      dynamic half of the AOT gate (tools/run-aot-tests.ps1, the `native-aot` job in ci.yml). The
      six suppressions below are scoped to this test project; src/FuzzyRegex carries none at all
      and, measured 2026-09-16 and again 2026-09-20, produces zero trim or AOT warnings of its own.

      IL2026, IL2070, IL2075 - this project reflects over the library on purpose. The thread-safety
      audits in Gaps/Api and the ported-test conventions scan read types, fields and properties by
      name to report what they find, so the trimmer cannot see how they are reached. The scans stay
      honest two ways: the assemblies they read are rooted below, and a scan that comes back empty
      now FAILS rather than passing over nothing - see
      ThreadSafetyTests.The_library_assembly_this_file_scans_has_not_been_trimmed_away and the
      count floor in PortedTestConventionsTests.Every_ported_test_in_this_assembly_conforms.

      IL2065 - S57, and the same reason one step further out. Conventions/PublicApiDocumentationTests
      walks Assembly.GetExportedTypes() and calls type.GetMembers(BindingFlags) on each, so the
      RECEIVER is a Type the trimmer cannot resolve statically; that is the whole point of the test,
      which exists to reflect over the real shipped surface rather than read the project's own
      PublicAPI.*.txt back to itself. This was the AOT gate's only error and it failed the publish
      (MSB3077); reproduced identically by S58, by S59 from a deleted obj/ and bin/, and again here
      on 2026-09-20 before the fix.

      Of the three ways out, annotating is not available: the value flows from GetExportedTypes(),
      which carries no DynamicallyAccessedMembers of its own, so a [DynamicallyAccessedMembers]
      helper parameter only moves the same warning to its call site. Excluding the one test from the
      native publish would delete AOT coverage of the convention that the shipped package is
      documented - the package is the thing AOT consumers get. So: suppressed, in this project's
      existing NoWarn list rather than as an UnconditionalSuppressMessage, because the neighbouring
      reflection warnings are already handled here and one mechanism is easier to audit than two.

      What makes it safe is measured, not asserted: FuzzyRegex is a TrimmerRootAssembly below, so
      its members are not trimmed, and the test now carries a member-count floor that fails if they
      ever are - the type and doc-entry floors it already had both survive trimming, and a count of
      members reflected does not. The native run is therefore the standing proof of this line.

      IL2104, IL3053 - AwesomeAssertions 9.6.0 is a FluentAssertions fork with reflection inside
      and is not marked AOT-compatible, so it reports these two aggregate warnings for the whole
      assembly. They cannot be fixed from here. What they mean in practice was measured rather than
      assumed: only BeEquivalentTo actually fails at run time, which is why S53 replaced its twelve
      call sites with Equivalence and banned it in AotAssertionConventionTests. The other ~4,600
      assertions run natively.
    -->
    <NoWarn>$(NoWarn);IL2026;IL2065;IL2070;IL2075;IL2104;IL3053</NoWarn>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!--
      S53. Root both assemblies rather than letting the trimmer keep only what it can see being
      called. The reflection-based audits above scan them by name, so a trimmed assembly would
      turn every one of those tests into a pass over nothing; and the library is the subject under
      test, not a dependency to be shaken down for size. The size claim the AOT gate makes for a
      real consumer is measured by samples/FuzzyRegex.AotSmoke instead, which roots nothing.
    -->
    <TrimmerRootAssembly Include="FuzzyRegex" />
    <TrimmerRootAssembly Include="FuzzyRegex.Tests" />
    <!--
      And TUnit.Assertions, because AwesomeAssertions reports a failure by reflectively
      constructing the host test framework's exception type -
      Activator.CreateInstance(typeof(TUnit.Assertions.Exceptions.AssertionException), message).
      The trimmer cannot see that call, so it drops the (string) constructor and the report comes
      out as "MissingMethodException: No parameterless constructor defined" instead of as the
      assertion that failed - measured 2026-09-16. The gate would still go red, but on a
      substituted error, which is worse than useless for diagnosing one.
    -->
    <TrimmerRootAssembly Include="TUnit.Assertions" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="TUnit" />
    <PackageReference Include="AwesomeAssertions" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Testing.Extensions.CodeCoverage" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="../../src/FuzzyRegex/FuzzyRegex.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!--
      S70. The demo's JSON contract, compiled into this assembly rather than referenced. The demo
      project targets browser-wasm and cannot be referenced from a net10.0 test project, but the
      contract is plain C# with no interop in it - the [JSExport] wrapper is a separate file - so
      linking the source pins it under the JIT on all three CI operating systems and the demo needs
      no test host of its own. Gaps/Demo/DemoEngineContractTests.cs is what reads it.
    -->
    <Compile Include="../../demo/FuzzyRegex.Demo.Wasm/DemoEngine.cs" Link="Gaps/Demo/DemoEngine.cs" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!--
      The compile-parity corpus, embedded rather than copied to the output directory so the tests
      never depend on the working directory. Regenerate with tools/record-compile-corpus.py.
    -->
    <EmbeddedResource Include="Gaps/CompileParity/corpus.json" />
    <!-- The Unicode table and casing digests. Regenerate with tools/record-unicode-fixtures.py. -->
    <EmbeddedResource Include="Gaps/Unicode/unicode-fixtures.json" />
    <!-- What the character-name table should be. Regenerate with tools/build-character-names.py. -->
    <EmbeddedResource Include="Gaps/Unicode/character-names.json" />
    <!-- What the lowercase table should be. Regenerate with tools/build-lowercase.py. -->
    <EmbeddedResource Include="Gaps/Unicode/lowercase.json" />
    <!--
      The demo sidebar's worked examples, embedded from the file the browser itself loads so the
      tour has one source (S71). Gaps/Demo/DemoExamplesTests.cs checks each one against upstream's
      answer; tools/probes/demo-examples-expectations.py is where those answers come from.
    -->
    <EmbeddedResource Include="../../demo/FuzzyRegex.Demo.Wasm/wwwroot/examples.json" Link="Gaps/Demo/examples.json" />
  </ItemGroup>
</Project>
